{
  "note": [
    "The Helicopter's fabric (DR-113), cut from the one tower layout in scripts/fabric-tower.mjs -- regenerate from there, do not hand-edit. HELICOPTER.metadata.json v3: a cabin 4.8 x 2.8 x 8.5 with its nose north, so this room's compass is the world's; forward is 0, the way out 270, the ocean 90 and the aft bulkhead 180. Its flank is cut 0.4 m thick, which is what makes the 4.8 m cabin the 5.6 m fuselage the Helipad sees, and gives each opening a vehicle's deep reveal.",
    "THE CABIN FLOOR IS 1.6 M OVER THE PAD DECK, and that number is what makes the game consistent. From the pad the fence hides everything; from in here the eye is 3.2 m up, 0.7 m ABOVE the 2.5 m fence, so every opening looks down over it and \"through the windows of the vehicle you can see a wide Helipad, and beyond that, endless ocean far below\" (compone.zil 2795-2800) is true. Three openings, gathered amidships on purpose: the west side doors as one 3.2 m gap on bearing 270 with their rusted leaves folded back aft, the east door 2 m on bearing 90 where the fence is nearest and the sea is a broad band, and the forward window band over the closed cover. No further aperture may be cut within 20 degrees of a seam, which is what rules out portholes fore and aft, and there is no tail ramp.",
    "Inside: tie-down plating, ribs over the empty bay, the complex control panel closed and locked on the forward bulkhead with its orange-key lock beside it -- shut in every state the game has, so no dial, screen, gauge or lit indicator is ever drawn. No cabin light of any kind: everything is lit through the three openings, by the same sun as the Helipad, bearing 235 and 35 degrees up, which comes in over the player's left shoulder as they look forward and leaves the cover in shade."
  ],
